1
00:00:03,500 --> 00:00:10,000
Hi, I'm Divyansh Agrawal. This is Diligence Room,
my entry for the Fortified Enterprise Fleet track.

2
00:00:10,500 --> 00:00:17,000
M&A due diligence is too complex for a single AI model.

3
00:00:17,500 --> 00:00:24,000
It needs a network of institutional agents that hook
directly into enterprise infrastructure.

4
00:00:24,500 --> 00:00:32,500
Human teams take weeks to check thousands of deal files.
I built a fleet of eight specialists that runs it
end to end, workstream by workstream.

5
00:00:33,500 --> 00:00:39,500
Operational Utility is forty percent of the score,
and it asks for real delegation, not a chat bot.

6
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:48,000
This is the central Agent Registry, where a manager can
discover, audit and manage eight cataloged specialists.

7
00:00:48,500 --> 00:00:56,500
Each owns one domain, with no shared raw access. Every
agent is versioned and scored, so a bad build rolls back.

8
00:00:57,500 --> 00:01:03,500
Visible proof of live execution. Left is my terminal
running the pipeline, right is the app on Cloud Run.

9
00:01:04,000 --> 00:01:09,000
Deals run for weeks, so the Agent Runtime handles
long-running async execution.

10
00:01:09,500 --> 00:01:14,500
I'm replaying fourteen days of deal events into Firestore,
the Memory Bank that holds context across sessions.

11
00:01:15,500 --> 00:01:22,000
Architectural Discipline, thirty percent, shows up
at the boundary.

12
00:01:22,500 --> 00:01:32,000
Before any agent sees a file, Gemma 4 parses the text and
Model Armor runs inline checks for prompt injection,
tool poisoning and PII leaks.

13
00:01:32,500 --> 00:01:41,000
Google ADK orchestrates the agents, Gemini 3.5 Flash
routes the work.

14
00:01:41,500 --> 00:01:51,000
To stop hallucinations, an agent cannot post a finding
unless it quotes the exact source text.

15
00:01:51,500 --> 00:02:00,000
Pub/Sub carries the messages, Cloud Trace logs every step.

16
00:02:00,500 --> 00:02:08,000
Look at the dashboard. Forty-nine events processed,
five findings flagged.

17
00:02:09,500 --> 00:02:16,000
The Legal agent spots a change-of-control clause
in a vendor contract.

18
00:02:16,500 --> 00:02:25,000
To size the risk it needs revenue data, but under Agent
Identity's zero-trust model it has no read access
to the finance ledgers.

19
00:02:25,500 --> 00:02:34,000
So it sends a request to the Agent Gateway, which enforces
policy and returns one safe aggregate.

20
00:02:34,500 --> 00:02:41,000
Eighteen point three percent of next year's revenue
sits in this single contract.

21
00:02:41,500 --> 00:02:46,500
When four sub-agents flag the same vendor,
the coordinator escalates to critical.

22
00:02:47,500 --> 00:02:56,000
A fortified fleet has to prove its security posture.
Twenty red-team attacks: prompt injection, encoded
payloads, data theft, illegal cross-agent writes.

23
00:02:56,500 --> 00:03:03,000
All twenty blocked, with zero false positives.

24
00:03:03,500 --> 00:03:10,500
Here an attacker fakes a deal approval, and Model Armor
quarantined it before it reached an agent runtime.

25
00:03:11,500 --> 00:03:19,000
What happens when an agent fails? Legal runs 2.4.
I publish 2.5.

26
00:03:19,500 --> 00:03:25,500
The shadow harness replays a golden set against it,
and it fails: 2.5 missed the contract clause.

27
00:03:26,000 --> 00:03:34,500
I roll back to 2.4. The version reverts, but the Memory
Bank partition stays intact.

28
00:03:35,500 --> 00:03:43,000
Finally, governance. The negotiation agent drafts the
letter, but only I can send it, and that approval lands
in Cloud Logging as a request against Cloud Run.

29
00:03:43,500 --> 00:03:51,000
Cloud Run in two regions, Firestore, Pub/Sub and
Vertex AI Agent Engine, all live on Google Cloud.

30
00:03:51,500 --> 00:03:59,000
And the same call in Cloud Trace, span by span, so an
auditor can follow any claim back to its source.
